    <h2 class="section-title">Life cycle and feasibility of active collection</h2>
    <p>A landfill operation has a fairly well defined life cycle. The life cycle has four phases (Figure 2):</p>

    <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-bottom: 30px;">
        <div style="width: 48%;">
            <h3 class="subsection-title">Phase I</h3>
            <p>Phase I is aerobic (oxygen is present). The output of the landfill is primarily nitrogen with some CO2 and hydrogen.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="width: 48%;">
            <h3 class="subsection-title">Phase III</h3>
            <p>Phase III you will see the CO2 starting to level off at around 50%, while the methane is increasing rapidly to about 50%. Nitrogen and hydrogen are down around 5%.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; margin-bottom: 30px;">
        <div style="width: 48%;">
            <h3 class="subsection-title">Phase II</h3>
            <p>Phase II (and subsequent phases are anaerobic (no oxygen present). Phase II produces mostly CO2 with lesser degrees of nitrogen and hydrogen.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="width: 48%;">
            <h3 class="subsection-title">Phase IV</h3>
            <p>Phase IV, nitrogen has leveled off to about 2-5% and CO2 and methane are fairly constant at about 50% each.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="diagram-container">
        <img src="placeholder_diagram_2.png" alt="Landfill phases diagram" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto;">
        <p class="figure-caption">Fig. 2. Landfill phases of operation (Graph courtesy of EPA)</p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top: 40px;">
        <h2 class="section-title">A number of factors can be useful in determining the feasibility of using landfill gases for power generation and on-line gas analysis.</h2>

        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Factor</th>
                    <th>Percentage</th>
                    <th>Notes</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>Methane</td>
                    <td>{{ methane_percentage }}%</td>
                    <td></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Carbon Dioxide</td>
                    <td>{{ co2_percentage }}%</td>
                    <td></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Nitrogen</td>
                    <td>{{ nitrogen_percentage }}%</td>
                    <td></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Oxygen</td>
                    <td>{{ oxygen_percentage }}%</td>
                    <td>Indicates under ground combustion</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Carbon Monoxide</td>
                    <td>{{ carbon_monoxide_percentage }}%</td>
                    <td></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>Hydrogen Sulfide</td>
                    <td>{{ hydrogen_sulfide_percentage }}%</td>
                    <td>Typically less than 1%</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
            <p>• Does the landfill hole more than 1 million tons of waste?</p>
            <p>• What is the waste material?</p>
            <p>• Biodegradable waste</p>
            <p>• Sewage treatment sludge (raw and/or secondary)</p>
            <p>• Commercial waste</p>
            <p>• Food waste</p>
            <p>• Organic component of mixed municipal waste</p>
            <p>• Biomass (like maize)</p>
            <p>• Does the landfill cover more than 40 acres?</p>
            <p>• Is the waste at least 35 feet deep?</p>
            <p>• Warm, moist climates are better suited than cool, dry climates.</p>
        </div>

        <div style="margin-top: 30px;">
            <p>• Is the energy user nearby?</p>
            <p>• Industrial guidelines suggest that gas recovery is feasible if the landfill produces 1 million cubic feet of gas per day.</p>
            <p>• Average landfill gases typically run about 500BTU/cubic foot. They may require blending before being sold to a local natural gas pipeline. Typical component break down for Landfill Applications:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>This is an abandoned landfill or is it still in use? Landfills tend to be most productive in their first 20 years of service</li>
                <li>What is the expected lifespan of the landfill?</li>
                <li>Where is your landfill with regard to its life cycle?</li>
            </ul>
            <p>The amount of energy that any specific landfill can produce will be dictated by many factors (size, age, materials in the landfill, climate, soil conditions, etc.), but typical installations tend to range between 3 and 8 megawatts. A 6 megawatt plant would produce about 1.5 million kilowatt-hours per year, enough to supply about 3,200 homes. Due to the cost of maintenance on this size of electric generation equipment, an on-line GC is usually easily justified.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

